The tool you are about to build will receive two arguments: the name of a file containing a secret message that needs encoding (the input), and the name of the file where the encoded message should be written (the output).

This version of the task is spy-themed. Your program should scan the message and apply the following transformations:

Every instance of (.) should replace the word before it with the hexadecimal version of that word. In this case, the word before (.) will always be a decimal number. (Ex: "Agent 30 (.) entered the room" -> "Agent 1e entered the room")

Every instance of (~) should encrypt the word before it into 8-bit binary ASCII. Each character in the word should become one 8-bit binary code, separated by spaces. (Ex: "HQ sent go (~)" -> "HQ sent 01100111 01101111")

Every instance of (?) should decrypt the binary code before it back into text. In this case, the word before (?) will always be an 8-bit binary ASCII code. (Ex: "HQ received 01000001 (?)" -> "HQ received A")

Every instance of (up) converts the word before it to uppercase. (Ex: "send the signal (up) !" -> "send the SIGNAL!")

Every instance of (low) converts the word before it to lowercase. (Ex: "The GUARD is watching (low)" -> "The guard is watching")

Every instance of (cap) converts the word before it to capitalized form. (Ex: "meet agent raven (cap)" -> "meet agent Raven")

For (low), (up), and (cap), if a number appears next to it, like so: (low, <number>), it changes the previously specified number of words. (Ex: "the safe is open (up, 3)" -> "the SAFE IS OPEN")

Every instance of the punctuation marks ., ,, !, ?, : and ; should be close to the previous word and spaced apart from the next one. (Ex: "The package is ready ,move now !!" -> "The package is ready, move now!!")

Except if there are groups of punctuation like ... or !?. In this case, the program should format the text as in the following example: "Wait ... The camera moved" -> "Wait... The camera moved".

The punctuation mark ' will always be found with another instance of it. They should be placed to the right and left of the words in the middle of them, without extra spaces. (Ex: "The password is ' shadow '" -> "The password is 'shadow'")

If there is more than one word between the two ' ' marks, the program should place the marks next to the corresponding words. (Ex: "The message says: ' meet me behind the station '" -> "The message says: 'meet me behind the station'")

Every instance of a should be turned into an if the next word begins with a vowel (a, e, i, o, u) or an h. (Ex: "A agent arrived late" -> "An agent arrived late")
